                "visibleText": "The read\nFix these first\nKeep these\nRole direction\nYOUR RESUME REVIEW\n\nTHE READ\n\nCLARITY SUMMARY: 88/100\n\nNot a prediction of interviews or offers.\n\nShow Tighten executive positioning\n\nDense outcome evidence makes the impact easy to verify; remaining work is mostly positioning.\n\nWHAT LANDS\n\nThe Novara role combines a 45-person organization, $25M budget, 2M+ users, and 40 countries, making the operating scale immediately visible.\n\nWHERE DOUBT CREEPS IN\n\nThe CMO role shows major commercial results, but it does not identify the company size, reporting relationship, or executive operating context.\n\nFIX FIRST\n\nAdd [reporting relationship] and [company revenue or employee scale] to the Novara leadership bullet.\n\nWhat we saw on the page: “- I led a 45-person global marketing organization managing a $25M annual budget, driving company-wide brand and demand generation strategy”\n\nTHE EVIDENCE\n\nThe specifics that support this read.\n\nSee all evidence\n\nFIX THESE FIRST\n\nThree moves. In order.\n\nEach one starts with evidence from the resume, then shows the context or proof that would make the story stronger.\n\nFIX 01\n\nStart here\n\nAdd [reporting relationship] and [company revenue or employee scale] to the Novara leadership bullet.\nNot relevant\n01\nON THE PAGE\n02\nOPEN QUESTION\n03\nYOUR FACT\n04\nCLEARER WORDING\n\nON THE PAGE · WORK EXPERIENCE\n\n“- I led a 45-person global marketing organization managing a $25M annual budget, driving company-wide brand and demand generation strategy”\n\nWHAT IS MISSING\n\nRecruiters can see the size of your function, but not the level of executive context in which you operated.\n\nANSWER BEFORE YOU EDIT\n\nAt Novara, which marketing decision most changed the path from 70% lead-flow growth to higher recurring revenue?\n\nVerified detail\nKeep these facts\n\nTRY THIS\n\nWe don’t invent accomplishments. Add every supporting fact below or use the factual no-number draft.\n\nEdit\nAdd facts to copy\n\nI led a 45-person global marketing organization managing a $25M annual budget, driving company-wide brand and demand generation strategy; [verified detail].\n\nFIX 02\n\nThen this\n\nAdd [primary program or channel] and [target audience] to the Senior Marketing Manager revenue bullet.\nNot relevant\n01\nON THE PAGE\n02\nOPEN QUESTION\n03\nYOUR FACT\n04\nCLEARER WORDING\n\nON THE PAGE · WORK EXPERIENCE\n\n“- I developed and executed integrated marketing programs for enterprise software solutions, contributing to a 25% uplift”\n\nWHAT IS MISSING\n\nThe result is useful, but the work behind it is too broad to distinguish your contribution from a general marketing remit.\n\nANSWER BEFORE YOU EDIT\n\nHow did you adapt marketing across 2M+ users and 40 countries without losing consistency?\n\nVerified detail\nKeep these facts\n\nTRY THIS\n\nWe don’t invent accomplishments. Add every supporting fact below or use the factual no-number draft.\n\nEdit\nAdd facts to copy\n\nI developed and executed integrated marketing programs for enterprise software solutions, contributing to a 25% uplift; [verified detail].\n\nFIX 03\n\nOne more pass\n\nAdd a summary section with [target role], [leadership scope], and [measurable result].\nNot relevant\n01\nON THE PAGE\n02\nOPEN QUESTION\n03\nYOUR FACT\n04\nCLEARER WORDING\n\nON THE PAGE · SUMMARY\n\n“No summary section present”\n\nWHAT IS MISSING\n\nWithout a summary, the recruiter must infer your intended lane from the chronology instead of seeing it immediately.\n\nANSWER BEFORE YOU EDIT\n\nWhat tradeoff did you manage while shortening launch time-to-market by 30%?\n\nVerified detail\nKeep these facts\n\nTRY THIS\n\nWe don’t invent accomplishments. Add every supporting fact below or use the factual no-number draft.\n\nEdit\nAdd facts to copy\n\nNo summary section present; [verified detail].\n\nKEEP THESE\n\nThe parts already earning trust.\n01\n\nThe Novara role combines a 45-person organization, $25M budget, 2M+ users, and 40 countries, making the operating scale immediately visible.\n\n02\n\nYour results span lead flow, recurring revenue, launch speed, acquisition cost, brand awareness, retention, pipeline, and win rates rather than one marketing metric.\n\n03\n\nThe progression from Senior Marketing Manager through VP to CMO is supported by increasing team and budget responsibility.\n\nROLE DIRECTION\n\nWhere this story reads strongest.\n\nPosition yourself first as an enterprise SaaS marketing executive with ownership of demand, brand, launches, and commercial growth. Choose whether the target is CMO or VP Marketing, then make the summary and skills order reinforce that lane.\n\nBEST FIT NOW\n\nChief Marketing Officer\nVP of Global Marketing\nVP of Demand Generation\n\nSeniority read: Executive-level marketing leader, based on the current CMO title, 45-person organization, and $25M budget.\n\nHow the clarity summary breaks down\n88/100\n\nAFTER YOU EDIT\n\nRun the new version. Compare the read.\n\nUpload the revised resume and we'll place the two opening reads side by side—what changed, what still needs context, and what to fix next.\n\nUnlock the second read\n\nAnother report requires paid access.\n\nA NOTE FOR THE BETA\n\nWhat did this report get right—or miss?\n\nThis is a small paid beta, and I read every note—especially the blunt ones. Tell me what felt useful, what felt off, and what nearly stopped you.\n\nSend a two-minute note\n\nThree prompts open in your email. Your resume is never attached.",
